    "text": "96\n\n The 7 Things You Must Check in a Purchase Agreement Before Buying an Accounting Firm\nHi Sara,\nIf you\u2019re buying an accounting practice, you\u2019re going to come across a purchase agreement\u2014and let\u2019s be real, it will probably look like a 20-page legal maze designed to put you to sleep. \nBut here\u2019s the thing: this document controls everything about your deal. It\u2019s your business\u2019s user manual, your playbook, and, if something goes wrong, your only protection.\nSo today, I\u2019m breaking down the seven key sections you must understand before signing on the dotted line.\n1. What Exactly Are You Buying? (And What\u2019s Excluded?)\nThis is where you find out if you\u2019re getting all the assets you expect\u2014or if the seller conveniently forgot to include key client lists, software, or equipment. If you\u2019re the buyer, you want the contract to say everything is included unless specifically excluded. If you\u2019re the seller? You want the opposite.\n2. The Money Details (a.k.a. The Purchase Price)\nHow much are you paying?\n\nIs it all cash, or is there a promissory note?\n\nAre there any earnout term or price adjustments based on client retention or revenue?\n\nMiss something here, and you might be in for a nasty surprise later.\n3. Closing Mechanics: When Does This Deal Actually Happen?\nMost deals aren\u2019t \u201csign today, close today.\u201d They involve a bifurcated sign-and-close, meaning:\n\u2714 You sign the agreement now.\n\u2714 The deal officially closes later\u2014after financing, approvals, or other conditions are met.\nIf you don\u2019t structure this properly, you could be legally obligated to buy before securing funding. (Yes, I\u2019ve seen this happen. Yes, it got ugly.)\n4. The Seller\u2019s Promises (And Why They Matter)\nReps and warranties are the seller\u2019s legal promises about the business. Things like:\n\u201cOur financial statements are accurate.\u201d\n\n\u201cWe actually own the assets we\u2019re selling.\u201d\n\n\u201cWe haven\u2019t secretly sold the client list to someone else.\u201d\n\nIf any of these turn out to be false, you\u2019ve got a breach of contract claim. But only if you made sure they were included in writing. These are SUPER important.\n5. What Happens If Something Goes Wrong?\nThis is where indemnification comes in\u2014it decides who pays if a lawsuit shows up later.\nExample: A client sues over work done before closing.\n\nWho\u2019s responsible\u2014you or the seller?\n\nGet this section wrong, and you could be on the hook for past problems you had nothing to do with.\n6. Non-Compete Clauses (The Fine Print That Matters)\nIf you\u2019re the buyer, you don\u2019t want the seller setting up shop across the street and poaching clients. If you\u2019re the seller, you don\u2019t want to be banned from your entire profession for the next decade.\nThe right non-compete is a balance\u2014clear, enforceable, and specific. If it\u2019s too broad, courts might throw it out. If it\u2019s too narrow, it won\u2019t protect you. If you\u2019re the one under a non-compete, you\u2019re going to want it to be specific enough that you can tell whether or not a business opportunity will violate it.\n7. The Boilerplate (Yes, You Still Have to Read It)\nThe last section might seem like legal fluff, but I\u2019ve found some wild clauses buried in here\u2014like secret fees, weird assignment rules, and even clauses that completely contradict earlier sections. So read it.\nThe Bottom Line\nA purchase agreement is more than just paperwork\u2014it\u2019s the foundation of your deal. If you don\u2019t understand what\u2019s in it, you\u2019re flying blind.\nIf you\u2019re buying an accounting firm and want to make sure your agreement is airtight, check out our courses at Deal Academy to get the support you need.\nThis is what I do every day, and trust me\u2014you don\u2019t want to find out what\u2019s missing in your contract when it\u2019s already too late.\nTo smarter deals,\nSara Sharp\nPartner, SK&S Law Group\nBusiness Consultant and Founder of Deal Academy\nP.S. If you're looking to buy or sell an accounting firm, and want me to keep an eye out for you, fill out this form here.\n\n For more helpful lessons from Deal Academy, check out our Dealmakers Youtube Channel where you'll find Sara's Weekly Wine Down, where she talks about what she's seen in the industry this week and interviews with M&A Experts and Dealmakers.\nYou can check out some recent Dealmakers podcast episodes by clicking the images below.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2025\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
    "html": "<div style=\"font-family: Roboto, Arial; font-size: 14px;\"><!DOCTYPE html><html lang=\"und\" dir=\"auto\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" xmlns:v=\"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:vml\" xmlns:o=\"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office\"><head>\n    <title></title>\n    <!--[if !mso]><!-->\n    <meta http-equiv=\"X-UA-Compatible\" content=\"IE=edge\">\n    <!--<![endif]-->\n    <meta http-equiv=\"Content-Type\" content=\"text/html; charset=UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n    <style type=\"text/css\">\n      #outlook a { padding:0; }\n      body { margin:0;padding:0;-webkit-text-size-adjust:100%;-ms-text-size-adjust:100%; }\n      table, td { border-collapse:collapse;mso-table-lspace:0pt;mso-table-rspace:0pt; }\n      img { border:0;height:auto;line-height:100%; outline:none;text-decoration:none;-ms-interpolation-mode:bicubic; }\n      p { display:block;margin:13px 0; }\n    </style>\n    <!--[if mso]>\n    <noscript>\n    <xml>\n    <o:OfficeDocumentSettings>\n      <o:AllowPNG/>\n      <o:PixelsPerInch>96</o:PixelsPerInch>\n    </o:OfficeDocumentSettings>\n    </xml>\n    </noscript>\n    <![endif]-->\n    <!--[if lte mso 11]>\n    <style type=\"text/css\">\n      .mj-outlook-group-fix { width:100% !important; }\n    </style>\n    <![endif]-->\n    \n      <!--[if !mso]><!-->\n        <link href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Ubuntu:300,400,500,700\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\">\n        <style type=\"text/css\">\n          @import url(https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Ubuntu:300,400,500,700);\n        </style>\n      <!--<![endif]-->\n\n    \n    \n    <style type=\"text/css\">\n      @media only screen and (min-width:480px) {\n        .mj-column-per-100 { width:100% !important; max-width: 100%; }\n      }\n    </style>\n    <style media=\"screen and (min-width:480px)\">\n      .moz-text-html .mj-column-per-100 { width:100% !important; max-width: 100%; }\n    </style>\